# CYC26 — The Claw: Minions of Toil

Working brief for the Commit Your Code 2026 talk. Title stays. Abstract is a draft.

## Slot

- Conference: [Commit Your Code 2026](https://www.commityourcode.com/) — Capital One Campus, Plano
- Agenda: [commityourcode.com/agenda](https://www.commityourcode.com/agenda)
- Title: **The Claw: Minions of Toil**
- Track: AI
- Time: Friday 2026-09-04, 2:00–2:25 pm (25 minutes)
- Machine contract: `data/talk.json`

Adjacent AI talks that afternoon:

- 1:30 — Michael Liendo, *Trust, But Verify: Human-in-the-Loop for Agents That Actually Matter*
- 2:30 — Kyle Taylor, *Your Prompt Is Not a Unit Test*

Room is a $40 six-track charity conference. Do not assume Effect, OpenClaw, herdr, or CascadiaJS context.

## Thesis

Swarms stay the frame. The zoom-in is the software the swarm works on.

Minions of toil only produce durable results when the cheap path is the honest path: written law, an enforceable fence, Effect at the door, receipts the next agent can read.

This repo is that floor. It was cloned from `joelhooks/ts-cli-template` on purpose.

## What this talk is not

Not a second Cascadia. Cascadia owns the field report: more agents was not the hard part; the harness owns freshness, permissions, approvals, side effects, receipts.

Not the AIE workshop. That is eight lessons. This slot is 25 minutes.

Not The Claw course and not a JoelClaw demo. Those claws are lineage. See [claw-lineage](../resources/claw-lineage.svx).

## 25-minute cut

1. Title is a joke. A swarm without a floor is more confident sludge.
2. Dillon's ELI5: effect make typescript good.
3. One Cascadia sentence: more agents was not the hard part.
4. The floor is a repo, not a prompt.
5. Show this clone. `pnpm cli talk data/talk.json`.
6. Stop.

If time dies, cut 3 before 5.

